@h1 Tables reference h1@

Everything @@@code @table code@@@ accepts: five authoring forms, the header and
caption controls, and the HTML each emits. @code table code@ is dispatched to a
dedicated renderer, so it does not follow the generic id/class allowlist — the
parameters below are the whole surface.

@h2 Which form? h2@

@table |rows [[You have, Use, Header comes from], [rows typed out as text, inline CSV, row 0 by default], [a collection + column order, schema-as-array, the schema keys], [a collection + custom labels, schema-as-record, the schema labels], [a collection and want auto columns, rows-by-ref, the first record's keys], [cells with links or emphasis, body form (row/col nodes), first row unless header false]]| |caption Pick a form| table@

@h2 Inline CSV h2@

Row 0 is the header by default; every later row is data.

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Client, Hours], [One to One, 62.75], [Couple, 0.75]]| |caption Practicum table@
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Practicum</caption><thead><tr><th>Client</th><th>Hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One to One</td><td>62.75</td></tr><tr><td>Couple</td><td>0.75</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/inline-csv.wit code@.

@h2 Schema-as-array (positional) h2@

Schema keys pick record fields @strong in order strong@ and become the headings;
every row is data (no header consumed).

@@@pre
#sites: [{ name - Dunmore Head, status - operational }, { name - Carrick Point, status - maintenance }] !!

@table |schema [name, status]| |rows @sites|
pre@@@

Emits headings @code name code@ / @code status code@ over the two records.
@strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/schema-array.wit code@.

@h2 Schema-as-record (labelled) h2@

The record @strong key strong@ selects the field; the record @strong value strong@
is the column heading.

@@@pre
@table |schema { name - Site, status - Status }| |rows [[Dunmore Head, operational], [Carrick Point, maintenance]]|
pre@@@

Emits headings @code Site code@ / @code Status code@ (not @code name code@ /
@code status code@). @strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/schema-record.wit code@.

@h2 Rows-by-ref (auto columns) h2@

Pass a collection of records as @code rows code@; columns are auto-derived from
the @strong first record's keys strong@. The renderer prefers the expander's
typed collection over literal text.

@@@pre
#sales: [ { region - West, total - 100 }, { region - East, total - 90 } ]

@table |rows @sales| table@
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><thead><tr><th>region</th><th>total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>West</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>East</td><td>90</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@aside(class note)
A @strong raw strong@ @@@code @@table code@@@ cannot resolve
@@@code |rows @sales| code@@@ — a raw body is a verbatim Text node, so
resolve and expand never run on it and there is no typed collection to derive
columns from. Use the ordinary @@@code @table code@@@. See
@a |href /docs/reference/limitations/| limitations a@.

aside@

@h2 Body form (row / col nodes) h2@

The only form whose cells hold @strong rich content strong@ — links, emphasis,
nested nodes — rather than plain strings. With no @code rows code@ parameter, the
renderer reads the body: @@@code @row code@@@ rows of @@@code @col code@@@ cells
(the HTML-style @@@code @tr code@@@ / @@@code @td code@@@ / @@@code @th code@@@
names work too).

@@@pre
@table |caption Splits|
  @row @col Segment col@ @col Notes col@ row@
  @row @col Ridge col@ @col A *steep* start; see @a |href /route| the route a@. col@ row@
table@
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Splits</caption><thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ridge</td><td>A <strong>steep</strong> start; see <a href="/route">the route</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

The @strong first row is the header strong@ (its cells render as
@@@code <th> code@@@) unless @code header false code@; a @@@code @th code@@@ cell
is always a header cell, even in a body row. Both the single-line form
(@@@code @row @col a col@ @col b col@ row@ code@@@) and the multi-line form above
produce the same table.

@@@code @row code@@@ and @@@code @col code@@@ are @strong interchangeable strong@ —
the outer node is the row, the inner one the cell — so
@@@code @col @row a row@ @row b row@ col@ code@@@ and
@@@code @row @col a col@ @col b col@ row@ code@@@ render identically
(@@@code @tr code@@@ / @@@code @td code@@@ / @@@code @th code@@@ work as aliases).

@h3 Generating rows from data h3@

Wrap the @@@code @row code@@@ lines in an @@@code (each …) code@@@ loop to build
the table from a collection — the one form that pairs data-driven rows with rich
cells. An @@@code (if …) code@@@ inside the loop filters which rows appear.

@@@pre
#stations: [ { name - Dunmore Head, status - operational }, { name - Ines Rock, status - operational } ]

@table |caption Stations|
  @row @col Site col@ @col Status col@ row@
  (each @stations as s)
  @row @col @s.name col@ @col @em @s.status em@ col@ row@
  (end)
table@
pre@@@

Data access resolves inside a cell @strong body strong@
(@@@code @col @s.name col@ code@@@), but @strong not inside a parameter strong@ — a
link's @@@code |href| code@@@ cannot be @@@code @s.url code@@@ yet, so keep
data-driven values in the cell body.

@h2 Header and caption controls h2@

@table |rows [[Param, Effect], [caption text, prepends a caption element], [header false, no header row], [header N, use row index N as the header], [header [a b c], explicit header cells — all rows become data], [default, row 0 of rows is the header]]| |caption Header controls| table@

@@@pre
@table |rows [[a, b], [c, d]]| |header false|
→ <table><tbody><tr><td>a</td><td>b</td></tr><tr><td>c</td><td>d</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/no-header.wit code@.

@h2 Edge cases and value rules h2@

@table |rows [[Case, Behaviour, Fixture], [empty or missing rows, empty table element, 19 empty], [multi-line cell content, preserved, 19 multiline-cell], [number or boolean cell, String(value), 19 tables], [null cell, empty cell, 19 tables], [comma inside a cell, splits the cell — avoid, verified], [quoting a cell, not supported inline — quotes stay literal, verified]]| |caption Edge cases| table@

@@@pre
@table table@ → <table></table>
pre@@@

@aside(class note)
Two boundaries worth knowing. @strong Inline cells cannot be quoted or
comma-escaped: strong@ a comma inside a cell splits it, and surrounding quotes
render literally. @strong CSV/TSV sources have no quoted-field handling
strong@ (the delimited-source parser), so commas or tabs inside a field break
rows there too; inline @@@code |rows [...]| code@@@ uses the real collection
parser and is unaffected by the source path.

aside@

@h2 Centering is theme, not renderer h2@

The default HTML pathway centres tables via
@@@code .wit-doc table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1em auto } code@@@ in
the theme. @@@code --raw code@@@ ships a reset-only base, so it does not centre —
add your own CSS. @strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/* code@ and the theme.

